          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582783,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Early Life of Louis Pasteur</strong><br>\nLouis Pasteur was born on December 27, 1822, in Dole, France. He was known for his incredible work in chemistry and microbiology. Pasteur was the son of a tanner, a profession which instilled in him a strong work ethic from a young age. He excelled academically and went on to earn his bachelor's degree in arts and a bachelor's degree in sciences from the Royal College of Besançon.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Education and Research</strong><br>\nAfter completing his early education, Louis Pasteur became a professor of chemistry at the University of Strasbourg. There, he met his wife, Marie Laurent, with whom he had five children. His research on molecular asymmetry in tartaric acid laid the foundation for the field of stereochemistry. He also studied the effects of microorganisms on the fermentation process, which was crucial for food safety.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Discovery of Pasteurization</strong><br>\nPasteur's research led to the breakthrough method known as pasteurization. He discovered that heating liquids like milk and wine just enough to kill most bacteria and molds prevented spoilage and diseases. This process was a significant advancement in food preservation and has been used worldwide since its development.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "53bc5361-5ce5-43a9-a00f-57bbceee9cd0"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
