          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582782,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Vaccination for Chicken Cholera</strong><br>\nPasteur's work extended beyond chemistry to the study of diseases. He developed a vaccine for chicken cholera after noticing that old bacterial cultures lost their virulence. By injecting weaker bacterial strains into chickens, he could prevent the onset of severe disease, marking the start of preventive vaccination.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Anthrax Vaccine</strong><br>\nBuilding on his vaccination method, Pasteur successfully created a vaccine for anthrax, a deadly disease affecting cattle. His demonstration of the vaccine's effectiveness in 1881 proved that controlled exposure to a weakened pathogen could protect against future infections and saved the French silk industry.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Rabies Vaccine</strong><br>\nOne of Pasteur's most famous achievements was developing the first rabies vaccine. He cultivated the virus in rabbits and then weakened it to create the vaccine. This work saved many lives, including that of a young boy bitten by a rabid dog, who was the first human to receive the vaccine.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "3b13867b-feab-4e2e-bfa2-fcc82e4dbff1"
            },
